# Resizing: the screen reflows, the cursor stays on its text.
#
# xterm itself does not reflow (it cuts and pads lines); this engine does, so these cases are
# conventions of the engine rather than of a published text. They pin down what must stay true: the text, its order and where the cursor is.
#
# One line is left as xterm would leave it when the screen gets wider: the one the cursor is on. The
# cases at the end say why, with the bytes zsh and bash write after a resize.

test widening joins a wrapped row
convention rows that were wrapped by autowrap are one line again when the screen is wide enough
size 5 4
feed abcdefgh\r\n
resize 10 4
screen
|abcdefgh|
cursor 2,1
wrapped 1 no

test narrowing keeps the text of a long row
convention a line longer than the new width wraps onto further rows; the text and its order do not change
size 10 4
feed abcdefghijkl
resize 5 4
lines
|abcdefghijkl|
cursor *,3

test narrowing keeps the rows on screen when they fit
convention rows that still fit the screen after reflow stay on it; they are not pushed into the history
known-fail: alacritty – reflow keeps the cursor on its screen row and pushes the rows above it into the history even when everything fits
size 10 4
feed abcdefghijkl
resize 5 4
screen
|abcde|
|fghij|
|kl|
cursor 3,3
wrapped 1 yes
wrapped 2 yes
wrapped 3 no
scrollback-len 0

test widening keeps rows that ended with a line break apart
convention only autowrapped rows are joined
size 5 4
feed ab\r\ncd
resize 10 4
lines
|ab|
|cd|
screen
|ab|
|cd|

test narrowing and widening again restores the line
convention reflow is lossless for plain text
size 10 4
feed abcdefghijkl\r\n
resize 5 4
resize 10 4
lines
|abcdefghijkl|
cursor *,1

test growing the height adds blank rows below
convention with no history the new rows are blank and the content stays where it was
size 10 2
feed A\r\nB
resize 10 4
screen
|A|
|B|
cursor 2,2

test shrinking the height keeps the cursor row on screen
convention lines above the cursor move into the history so that the cursor row stays visible
size 10 4
feed A\r\nB\r\nC\r\nD
resize 10 2
screen
|C|
|D|
cursor 2,2
scrollback
|A|
|B|

test the cursor stays inside after narrowing
convention the cursor is clamped to the new size
size 10 4
feed \e[1;10H
resize 5 4
cursor 1,5

test resizing keeps the rendition of the text
convention reflow keeps the cell attributes with the text
size 5 4
feed \e[31mabcdefg\r\n
resize 10 4
cell 1,7 text=g fg=idx:1
cell 1,1 text=a fg=idx:1

test resizing does not leave the alternate screen
convention resize is not a mode change
size 10 4
feed \e[?1049h
resize 6 3
alt-screen on
feed \e[?1049l
alt-screen off

test resizing resets the scrolling region
convention a resize returns the margins to the whole screen, as DECSLPP/DECSCPP do
size 10 4
feed \e[2;3r
resize 10 6
feed \e[6;1H\n
scrollback-len 1

test wide characters are not split by narrowing
convention a wide character that does not fit at the end of a row moves to the next one whole
size 6 4
feed ab日cd
resize 3 4
lines
|ab日cd|

test resize on the alternate screen leaves it without history
spec xterm ctlseqs: the alternate screen buffer has no scrollback (CSI ? 1049 h)
size 5 3
feed \e[?1049h
resize 5 4
feed 1\n2\n3\n4\n5\n6\n
scrollback-len 0

# The line the cursor is on. zsh's line editor and readline remember on which row of their own text
# the cursor is. Told of a new size they move up that many rows and draw again, as on a terminal
# that does not reflow; with the rows above the cursor joined they end in the line over the prompt
# and erase it. The bytes below are what zsh 5.9 and bash 3.2 wrote in a pseudo-terminal that went
# from 35 to 103 columns, under a prompt of 63 cells.

test widening leaves the rows of the line the cursor is on
convention on a wider screen the rows of the cursor's line stay rows, each a line of its own, so the cursor is as many rows under the first of them as the program that drew them remembers
size 5 4
feed abcdefgh
resize 10 4
screen
|abcde|
|fgh|
cursor 2,4
wrapped 1 no
lines
|abcde|
|fgh|

test widening leaves a full last row of the cursor's line with the cursor after it
convention the cursor that waited after the last column of its row stands after the text on the wider screen
size 5 4
feed abcdefghij
resize 10 4
screen
|abcde|
|fghij|
cursor 2,6

test widening joins the wrapped lines above and below the cursor's
convention only the line the cursor is on is left as it is
size 5 6
feed abcdefgh\r\nijklmnop\r\nqrstuvwx\e[3;2H
resize 10 6
screen
|abcdefgh|
|ijklm|
|nop|
|qrstuvwx|
cursor 2,2

test narrowing rewraps the line the cursor is on
convention a narrower screen cuts no text: the cursor's line wraps onto more rows like any other and the cursor stays on its character
size 10 4
feed abcdefghijkl\e[2;1H
resize 5 4
screen
|abcde|
|fghij|
|kl|
cursor 3,1
wrapped 1 yes
wrapped 2 yes

test zsh draws its wrapped prompt again on a wider screen and the line above it stays
convention zsh goes to the first column, up the one row its prompt had above the cursor, erases to the end of the screen and writes the prompt
size 35 5
feed above-the-prompt\r\n
repeat 61 p
feed %\s
cursor 3,29
resize 103 5
cursor 3,29
feed \r\r\e[A\e[0m\e[27m\e[24m\e[J
repeat 61 p
feed %\s
screen
|above-the-prompt|
|ppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppp%|
cursor 2,64

test zsh draws a command typed over three rows again on a wider screen and the line above it stays
convention zsh goes up the two rows its prompt and command had above the cursor, in two steps, before it erases and writes both
size 35 6
feed above-the-prompt\r\n
repeat 61 p
feed %\sls -la some-file
cursor 4,10
resize 103 6
feed \e[A\r\r\e[A\e[0m\e[27m\e[24m\e[J
repeat 61 p
feed %\sls -la some-file\r\r\n\e[K\e[A\e[79C
screen
|above-the-prompt|
|ppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppp% ls -la some-file|
cursor 2,80

test bash draws its wrapped prompt again on a wider screen and the line above it stays
convention readline goes to the first column, erases the row, goes up the one row its prompt had above the cursor and writes the prompt over it
size 35 5
feed above-the-prompt\r\n
repeat 61 p
feed %\s
resize 103 5
feed \r\e[K\e[A
repeat 61 p
feed %\s
screen
|above-the-prompt|
|ppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppppp%|
cursor 2,64
